# To configure a testbed machine

1. Copy the files that are on disk to /root

$ su - root
...

# mcopy a:*.* /root

2. Copy the resolv.conf file to /etc

# cp /root/resolv.conf /etc/resolv.conf

3. Cat the passwd.testbed file to the existing one, first checking that the
existing password file does not contain the test bed accounts.
The passowrd file should look like:

root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash
bin:x:1:1:bin:/bin:
daemon:x:2:2:daemon:/sbin:
adm:x:3:4:adm:/var/adm:
lp:x:4:7:lp:/var/spool/lpd:
sync:x:5:0:sync:/sbin:/bin/sync
shutdown:x:6:0:shutdown:/sbin:/sbin/shutdown
halt:x:7:0:halt:/sbin:/sbin/halt
mail:x:8:12:mail:/var/spool/mail:
news:x:9:13:news:/var/spool/news:
uucp:x:10:14:uucp:/var/spool/uucp:
operator:x:11:0:operator:/root:
games:x:12:100:games:/usr/games:
gopher:x:13:30:gopher:/usr/lib/gopher-data:
ftp:x:14:50:FTP User:/home/ftp:
nobody:x:99:99:Nobody:/:

(or with the encrypted passwords in case there is no /etc/shadow)

# cat passwd.testbed >> /etc/passwd

If shadow passwords are being used, remember to do

# pwconv

so that the shadow file gets updated.

4. Create the accounts

# cat passwd.test | ./accounts.pl | /bin/sh

4. Install ssh

# tar xvzf ssh-1.2.27.tar.gz
# cd ssh-1.2.27
# ./configure; make; make install

5. Add the following two lines at the end of /etc/rc.d/rc.local
source /root/firewall.sh
/usr/local/sbin/sshd &

# echo "source /root/firewall.sh" >> /etc/rc.d/rc.local; 
# echo "/usr/local/sbin/sshd &" >> /etc/rc.d/rc.local; 


